          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stael-corinne-5009c0d40dfc5c97\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Oswald directly avows his present, exceptionally intense love for Corinne and invokes it as a reason she should not fear losing him. This strongly supports T. The surrounding speech moves toward lifelong commitment, but the highlighted clause itself primarily presents his love as an existing state rather than independently undertaking an obligation, so P receives only slight support. Neither the hyperbolic “avec idolâtrie” nor the emotional situation shows that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge, so E is unsupported.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The broader turn includes explicit commitment (“je suis à toi pour toujours”) and imagined life companionship, creating limited P pressure, but that force is not clearly encoded by the highlighted love clause itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Lord Nelvil directly addresses Corinne; “je vous aime” is embedded in his rhetorical question asking what she can fear when he loves her with idolatrous devotion.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Corinne reacts with near-fainting to the possibility that Oswald might marry Lucile. Oswald uses his love avowal to reassure her, then explains that his father’s wish did not bind him and declares himself hers forever. Corinne’s concealed history makes his confidence tragically precarious, but that narrative complication does not alter the clause’s principally truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“que pouvez-vous craindre de moi, Corinne, quand je vous aime avec idolâtrie?”\",\"supports\":\"The present-tense clause explicitly presents Oswald’s intense love as true and uses it as the premise of reassurance, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Oswald explains that he made no engagement with Lucile’s mother and says Corinne has triumphed over his former wish to fulfill his father’s preference.\",\"supports\":\"His avowal is part of a reasoned assurance that his love for Corinne supersedes the contemplated marriage, rather than an unmediated reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“je suis à toi pour toujours, à toi!” followed by his imagining Corinne as the companion of his life.\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding speech supplies genuine commitment-oriented context and therefore slight P support, while distinguishing that undertaking from the highlighted love clause.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“avec idolâtrie” intensifies the professed love.\",\"supports\":\"This is evidence of emphatic emotional intensity, but not independently of expressive/reflexive core force; it therefore does not support E above zero.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal, with a small amount of P pressure supplied by its place in a commitment-laden reassurance. Intensity and dramatic circumstances remain contextual and do not require E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
